00:32:35.800 --> 00:32:54.260
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program, White Haze. We try to make sense of all those overlapping groups that marched in Charlottesville, the fuzzy mix of alt-right and alt-light, and all the others that were there, and how people who say that they are not racist and not white supremacists have ended up in a melting pot with people who are.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目，“白色迷雾”。我们试图理清那些在夏洛茨维尔游行的所有重叠团体的含义，即“另类右翼”和“另类轻量级”的模糊混合，以及所有在那里的其他人，以及那些说他们不是种族主义者和不是白人至上主义者的人，最终如何与那些是的人混在了一个大熔炉里。

00:32:54.260 --> 00:33:25.100
<v Ira Glass>One of the things that we wondered here on our show after Charlottesville is why are there so many of these little groups? Like, why are there a lot of them? Why are they small? Like, why isn't there some big umbrella organization to advance their interests? And we were surprised to learn that for decades in the white power movement, this was a strategy. It was an actual strategy. And it was put into place in the 1980s. The strategy was called "leaderless resistance." And the white power movement adopted it because the FBI and federal law enforcement were successfully planting informants and covert agents inside the ranks.
<v Ira Glass>在夏洛茨维尔之后，我们在节目中想知道的一件事是，为什么有这么多这些小团体？比如，为什么有很多？为什么它们很小？比如，为什么没有某个大的伞形组织来推进他们的利益？我们很惊讶地了解到，在白人权力运动中，几十年来，这是一种策略。这是一种实际的策略。它在20世纪80年代被付诸实施。这种策略被称为“无领袖抵抗”。白人权力运动采用了它，因为联邦调查局和联邦执法部门成功地在队伍中安插了线人和卧底特工。

00:33:25.100 --> 00:33:38.390
<v Kathleen Belew>So leaderless resistance is a model of cell terrorism in which each activist and cell is supposed to act without direct communication with any other cell or with movement leadership.
<v Kathleen Belew>所以无领袖抵抗是一种细胞恐怖主义模式，其中每个活动家和细胞应该在没有与任何其他细胞或运动领导层直接沟通的情况下行动。

00:33:38.390 --> 00:33:56.510
<v Ira Glass>Kathleen Belew is a historian at the University of Chicago, and she's written about this period in the '70s and '80s and the white power movement for a book that's coming out next year. She says with leaderless resistance, people all over the country were reading the same propaganda, the same kind of playbook, if you will. But they all acted on their own. They were uncoordinated.
<v Ira Glass>凯瑟琳·贝莱（Kathleen Belew）是芝加哥大学的历史学家，她为明年出版的一本书写了关于70和80年代白人权力运动的这段时期。她说有了无领袖抵抗，全国各地的人们都在阅读同样的宣传，同样的剧本，如果你愿意的话。但他们都是自己行动的。他们是不协调的。

00:33:56.510 --> 00:34:09.080
<v Ira Glass>She says the idea for leaderless resistance was popularized by a man named Louis Beam, who was in the Klan and in the group Aryan Nations in 1983. She says we know how he sent out the memo to the rest of the movement on this.
<v Ira Glass>她说无领袖抵抗的想法是由路易斯·比姆（Louis Beam）普及的，他在1983年是三K党和雅利安国（Aryan Nations）团体的成员。她说我们知道他是如何向运动的其他部分发送关于这个的备忘录的。

00:34:09.080 --> 00:34:17.840
<v Kathleen Belew>There's an essay called Leaderless Resistance that's published in a collection of essays called Essays of a Klansman. So it was printed in '83, '89.
<v Kathleen Belew>有一篇名为“无领袖抵抗”的文章发表在一本名为《三K党人文章》的文集中。所以它是在83年，89年印刷的。

00:34:17.840 --> 00:34:26.720
<v Ira Glass>So he publishes it in a book. Is there also, like, a meeting where they all come together, and they discuss, and they take a vote, and they're like, "yes, that's what we're going to do."
<v Ira Glass>所以他在书中发表了它。是否还有一个会议，在那里他们聚在一起，讨论，进行投票，他们像，“是的，那就是我们要做的。”

00:34:26.720 --> 00:34:56.550
<v Kathleen Belew>So whether there is a vote is not clear, but there is a meeting. There is a meeting called the Aryan Nations World Congress. And this had been happening since the 1970s at the Hayden Lake compound in Idaho, which is where Aryan Nations is located. And the World Congress is both a political meeting and a social meeting. So there are fiery speeches about any number of causes important to this movement, and then there's also a big spaghetti dinner and volleyball games. At the meeting--
<v Kathleen Belew>所以是否有投票不清楚，但有一个会议。有一个会议叫雅利安国世界大会。自20世纪70年代以来，这一直发生在爱达荷州海登湖的复合体中，那是雅利安国所在地。世界大会既是政治会议也是社交会议。所以有关于这个运动重要的任何数量事业的激烈演讲，然后还有大意大利面晚餐和排球比赛。在会议上——

