00:40:23.800 --> 00:40:40.390
<v Robyn Semien>I had told him I wanted to talk about the rally and the events leading up to the rally, but I only realized sitting in front of him that I assumed he knew that meant talking about the man who organized the rally and his history with him. I ran that Kessler quote by him, or where Kessler said he organized the rally because of Bellamy.
<v Robyn Semien>我告诉他我想要谈论集会以及导致集会的事件，但我坐在他面前才意识到我假设他知道这意味着谈论组织集会的那个人以及他与他的历史。我把他提到的那篇凯斯勒引述给了他，或者说凯斯勒说他组织集会是因为贝拉米。

00:40:40.390 --> 00:40:43.830
<v Robyn Semien>He said, "I didn't do it. Wes Bellamy did it."
<v Robyn Semien>他说，“我没做。韦斯·贝拉米做的。”

00:40:43.830 --> 00:40:48.730
<v Wes Bellamy>All right. I didn't see that. I don't really, again, pay attention, a lot of attention to what he says, but--
<v Wes Bellamy>好的。我没看到那个。我不真的，再说一遍，注意，非常注意他说的话，但是——

00:40:48.730 --> 00:40:51.760
<v Robyn Semien>Why would you say that?
<v Robyn Semien>你为什么会这么说？

00:40:51.760 --> 00:41:34.470
<v Wes Bellamy>They, he and his posse, they firmly believe that I am-- I mean, they've said this on several occasions. I'm a huge threat to the quote, unquote, "white race." I'm a threat to them having what is theirs. Like, I'm taking it away. I'm a threat to what they believe to be the pure white race and Western civilization, and the way in which they do things. I'm a black male, who's 30, who is very vocal, who is in this position, who has a lot of support. And if that scares them, if that makes them believe this is my fault, that just, like, shows what kind of people they are. You have to take accountability. Wes Bellamy didn't plan an alt-right rally.
<v Wes Bellamy>他们，他和他的同伙，他们坚定地相信我是——我的意思是，他们在几次场合说过这个。我是对所谓的“白人种族”的巨大威胁。我是他们拥有属于他们东西的威胁。比如，我正在拿走它。我是对他们相信是纯白种族和西方文明的威胁，以及他们做事的方式。我是一个30岁的黑人男性，非常直言不讳，处于这个位置，拥有很多支持。如果这吓到了他们，如果这让他们相信这是我的错，那只是，就像，展示了他们是什么样的人。你必须承担责任。韦斯·贝拉米没有计划一场另类右翼集会。

00:41:34.470 --> 00:41:45.520
<v Robyn Semien>He was candid and had lots to say, until I mentioned I'd be talking to Kessler for my story, and then his whole body changed. He looked annoyed, stopped looking me in the eye.
<v Robyn Semien>他很坦率，有很多话要说，直到我提到我会为我的故事和凯斯勒谈话，然后他的整个身体都变了。他看起来很烦，不再看着我的眼睛。

00:41:45.520 --> 00:41:49.230
<v Robyn Semien>I'm talking to Kessler tomorrow, and that's fine.
<v Robyn Semien>我明天和凯斯勒谈话，那很好。

00:41:49.230 --> 00:41:57.240
<v Wes Bellamy>You're gonna put us in a story together? You didn't tell me that. OK. So we got 10 minutes. That's it.
<v Wes Bellamy>你要把我们放在一起做一个故事？你没告诉我那个。好的。所以我们有10分钟。就这样。

00:41:57.240 --> 00:41:59.760
<v Robyn Semien>Can I come back to you after, if I have more questions after him?
<v Robyn Semien>我可以在那之后回来找你吗，如果他在那之后我有更多问题的话？

00:41:59.760 --> 00:42:29.060
<v Wes Bellamy>Probably not, probably not. It's no disrespect intended. I just-- yo, this is way bigger than Kessler. This is way bigger than, like, Kessler. And I normally don't even spend-- I don't give him, like, that time. I don't think you quite understand. When you give these individuals that kind of time, that legitimizes them. That's what they want is the attention.
<v Wes Bellamy>可能不会，可能不会。没有任何不尊重。我只是——哟，这比凯斯勒大得多。这比，比如，凯斯勒大得多。我通常甚至不会花——我不给他，比如，那种时间。我不认为你完全理解。当你给这些人那种时间时，那使他们合法化了。他们想要的就是注意。

00:42:29.060 --> 00:43:10.960
<v Robyn Semien>I get that. But ignoring Jason Kessler isn't going to make him go away. And I don't have the same job as Wes Bellamy. He's a politician with a political vision for Charlottesville. It makes sense for him to avoid talking about Jason Kessler. He's not going to be derailed by one guy. But I'm in Charlottesville because Jason Kessler wrote the blueprint for the rally. And at this point, he's in hiding somewhere, presumably planning something. I wanted to know what's coming and how dangerous he is. And if this story was true, that Jason Kessler's beef with this one guy motivated him to organize this rally, I wanted to understand that too.
<v Robyn Semien>我明白那个。但忽视杰森·凯斯勒并不会让他消失。我没有和韦斯·贝拉米一样的工作。他是一个对夏洛茨维尔有政治愿景的政客。他避免谈论杰森·凯斯勒是有道理的。他不会被一个人破坏。但我在夏洛茨维尔因为杰森·凯斯勒写了集会的蓝图。在这一点上，他躲在某个地方，大概在计划什么。我想知道什么要来了，以及他有多危险。如果这个故事是真的，杰森·凯斯勒对这个人的不满促使他组织了这次集会，我也想理解那个。

00:43:10.960 --> 00:43:35.250
<v Robyn Semien>Before I spoke to Kessler, I went back and forth on telling him I'm black. I really chewed on it, wondered what was more honest. Tell him or don't tell him? Maybe he'll look me up. Maybe he'll ask me. I didn't know. It'll come up, I thought. And I don't usually start interviews with "hi, I'm black Robyn from This American Life." So I didn't do it with this one either.
<v Robyn Semien>在和凯斯勒谈话之前，我反复权衡是否要告诉他我是黑人。我真的咀嚼了它，想知道什么更诚实。告诉他还是不告诉他？也许他会查我。也许他会问我。我不知道。我觉得它会出现。而且我通常不以“嗨，我是来自《美国生活》的黑人罗宾”开始采访。所以我这次也没这样做。

00:43:35.250 --> 00:43:38.260
<v Robyn Semien>I've been reading that you're in hiding.
<v Robyn Semien>我一直在读你在躲藏。

00:43:38.260 --> 00:43:54.930
<v Jason Kessler>Well, I'm not announcing where I'm at. [CHUCKLING] I mean, when you have a government which won't protect you, you know, from mob violence, you can't just announce where you're going to be every moment.
<v Jason Kessler>好吧，我不宣布我在哪。（轻笑）我的意思是，当你有一个不会保护你的政府，你知道，免受暴民暴力，你不能只在每一刻宣布你要去哪里。

00:43:54.930 --> 00:43:57.300
<v Robyn Semien>Are you saying you've been getting threats?
<v Robyn Semien>你是说你一直受到威胁吗？

00:43:57.300 --> 00:44:07.080
<v Jason Kessler>Yeah, I've been getting constant death threats, constant lewd stuff, constant, you know, abuse from people.
<v Jason Kessler>是的，我一直受到不断的死亡威胁，不断的淫秽的东西，不断的，你知道，来自人们的虐待。

00:44:07.080 --> 00:44:17.770
<v Robyn Semien>I wanted to ask him about Wes Bellamy, but we got on the phone and he just started going. He talked for 40 minutes, mostly about how he did have a plan for the rally, but it was thwarted by the state of emergency.
<v Robyn Semien>我想问他关于韦斯·贝拉米的事，但我们通话，他就直接开始了。他谈了40分钟，主要谈论他确实有一个集会计划，但它被紧急状态破坏了。

00:44:17.770 --> 00:44:19.470
<v Jason Kessler>--the Constitution does not apply to me.
<v Jason Kessler>——宪法不适用于我。

00:44:19.470 --> 00:44:24.450
<v Robyn Semien>His First Amendment right to free speech was violated. There was a lot of First Amendment talk.
<v Robyn Semien>他支持言论自由的第一修正案权利被侵犯了。有很多关于第一修正案的谈话。

00:44:24.450 --> 00:44:27.090
<v Jason Kessler>Our First Amendment rights--
<v Jason Kessler>我们的第一修正案权利——

00:44:27.090 --> 00:44:42.850
<v Robyn Semien>He was rambly and excitable, and he talked over me a lot. And when I finally got to ask him about Wes Bellamy and that quote, he was even more hopped up. Bellamy is against him, but it's not just Bellamy. It goes all the way up to the highest levels of Virginia government.
<v Robyn Semien>他语无伦次且易怒，他经常打断我的话。当我终于问他关于韦斯·贝拉米和那个引述时，他更加兴奋了。贝拉米反对他，但不仅仅是贝拉米。这一直上达弗吉尼亚政府的最高层。

00:44:42.850 --> 00:44:49.580
<v Jason Kessler>I don't believe that it was the police that were trying to mess up the rally. I think that it was--
<v Jason Kessler>我不相信是警察试图搞乱集会。我认为是——

00:44:49.580 --> 00:44:49.890
<v Robyn Semien>You don't?
<v Robyn Semien>你不相信？

00:44:49.890 --> 00:44:57.640
<v Jason Kessler>--government officials, including mayor Mike Signer, Vice Mayor Wes Bellamy, and Virginia Governor Terry McAuliffe.
<v Jason Kessler>——政府官员，包括市长迈克·辛格尔（Mike Signer），副市长韦斯·贝拉米，和弗吉尼亚州州长特里·麦考利夫（Terry McAuliffe）。

00:44:57.640 --> 00:45:25.140
<v Robyn Semien>I asked him more questions about Wes Bellamy until it became clear-- there wasn't much there. Kessler's resentment of the vice mayor didn't prompt him to organize the rally. That's just something he said. But we did talk about the rally, and his plans and expectations, which eventually led to something that was new to me, something specific about his beliefs that felt important to know.
<v Robyn Semien>我问了他更多关于韦斯·贝拉米的问题，直到很清楚——那里没什么东西。凯斯勒对副市长的怨恨并没有促使他组织集会。那只是他说的东西。但我们确实谈论了集会，以及他的计划和期望，这最终导致了我不知道的事情，关于他的信仰的特定事情，觉得有必要知道。

00:45:25.140 --> 00:45:33.990
<v Robyn Semien>When President Trump stood before the press after the weekend in Charlottesville, he said not everyone at the rally was a neo-Nazi. Not everyone was a white supremacist.
<v Robyn Semien>当特朗普总统在夏洛茨维尔周末之后站在媒体面前时，他说不是集会上的每个人都是新纳粹。不是每个人都是白人至上主义者。

00:45:33.990 --> 00:45:36.830
<v Donald Trump>Believe me, Not all of those people were white supremacists.
<v Donald Trump>相信我，那些人并非全都是白人至上主义者。

00:45:36.830 --> 00:46:08.400
<v Robyn Semien>But even the guys who don't call themselves white supremacists believe all sorts of things that sound very close to or exactly the same as white supremacy. Jason, for instance, explained to me he's not a white supremacist. He's pro-white, fighting for white rights. And he organized one of the largest gatherings of white supremacists in recent memory. And he said things to me like, "each race has something to be proud of. Black people's thing being--" mhm-- "basketball."
<v Robyn Semien>但即使是那些不称自己为白人至上主义者的人，也相信各种听起来非常接近或完全等同于白人至上主义的事情。例如，杰森向我解释他不是白人至上主义者。他是亲白人的，为白人权利而战。他组织了最近记忆中最大的白人至上主义者集会之一。他对我说了一些话，比如，“每个种族都有值得骄傲的东西。黑人的东西是——”嗯——“篮球。”

00:46:08.400 --> 00:46:16.380
<v Robyn Semien>Were you on Friday night, on the Friday night before, were you out with that crowd marching, the guys with the torches?
<v Robyn Semien>你在周五晚上，在那之前的周五晚上，你和那个人群游行了吗，那些举着火把的家伙？

00:46:16.380 --> 00:46:17.100
<v Jason Kessler>Yes.
<v Jason Kessler>是的。

00:46:17.100 --> 00:46:18.460
<v Robyn Semien>You were marching with them?
<v Robyn Semien>你和他们一起游行了？

00:46:18.460 --> 00:46:19.200
<v Jason Kessler>Uh-huh.
<v Jason Kessler>嗯。

00:46:19.200 --> 00:46:26.220
<v Robyn Semien>And that was, like, a good representation of the group who you wanted at the rally the next day?
<v Robyn Semien>那是，比如，你第二天想要在集会上看到的那种群体的良好代表吗？

00:46:26.220 --> 00:46:42.270
<v Jason Kessler>Well, not all of it. I mean, there are elements of the online community which are into edgy humor. And I would say that some of the chants and stuff that they did were a little bit-- were in bad taste.
<v Jason Kessler>好吧，不是全部。我的意思是，网络社区中有喜欢前卫幽默的元素。我想说他们喊的一些口号之类的东西有点——品味很差。

00:46:42.270 --> 00:46:44.750
<v Robyn Semien>Yeah, what was the edgy humor?
<v Robyn Semien>是的，前卫幽默是什么？

00:46:44.750 --> 00:47:02.030
<v Jason Kessler>Well, I think when people say "Jews will not replace us," I think that they're taking an unhappiness with the disproportionate influence and power of Jewish people at the elite levels of power in American society. They're vastly overrepresented.
<v Jason Kessler>好吧，我认为当人们说“犹太人不会取代我们”时，我认为他们是对犹太人在美国社会精英阶层中不成比例的影响力和权力感到不满。他们被严重高估了。

00:47:02.030 --> 00:47:17.960
<v Robyn Semien>So when the media was reporting that people with white nationalist or white supremacist symbology were carrying torches and shouting "Jews will not replace us," you were thinking, "the media's getting it wrong because they're just-- it's a joke."
<v Robyn Semien>所以当媒体报道说有白人民族主义或白人至上主义符号的人举着火把喊“犹太人不会取代我们”时，你就在想，“媒体搞错了，因为他们只是——这只是个笑话。”

00:47:17.960 --> 00:47:39.880
<v Jason Kessler>What is the principle on which you oppose using torches? It was a form of protest. It was legal. It was permitted by free speech. And it is a good optic. It looks interesting at night.
<v Jason Kessler>你反对使用火把的原则是什么？这是一种抗议形式。这是合法的。它是被言论自由允许的。而且这是一个好的视觉效果。它在晚上看起来很有趣。

00:47:39.880 --> 00:48:04.250
<v Robyn Semien>Kessler's a UVA grad. He's 33. He voted for Obama. And when he talks about what set him down this path to pro-white activism, he points to a single experience. I was surprised what a common experience it was, something that happens to people all the time, and they do not end up organizing rallies with torches. He didn't get a job he wanted. Here's what happened.
<v Robyn Semien>凯斯勒是弗吉尼亚大学的毕业生。他33岁。他投了奥巴马的票。当他谈论是什么让他走上这条亲白人激进主义的道路时，他指向了一个单一的经历。我惊讶于这是一种多么普遍的经历，这种事情人们一直都在发生，而他们并没有最终组织举着火把的集会。他没得到他想要的工作。这是发生了什么。

00:48:04.250 --> 00:48:17.130
<v Robyn Semien>He didn't come from money. His parents didn't go to college. He graduated with a psych degree and student loans, and started looking for a job. He says he'd been working some. Says for about a year he was a counselor in a mental health facility.
<v Robyn Semien>他不是来自有钱人家。他的父母没上过大学。他毕业于心理学学位和学生贷款，开始找工作。他说他一直工作了一些。说是大约一年他是一个心理健康机构的咨询师。

00:48:17.130 --> 00:48:24.630
<v Jason Kessler>So I had experience, and I tried to apply for a job at Region 10, which is the health care provider around here.
<v Jason Kessler>所以我有了经验，我试着申请Region 10的工作，那是这里周围的医疗服务提供者。

00:48:24.630 --> 00:48:24.900
<v Robyn Semien>Mhm.
<v Robyn Semien>嗯。

00:48:24.900 --> 00:48:31.630
<v Jason Kessler>And there were two positions. There was one position, which was full time, and one which was as-needed.
<v Jason Kessler>有两个职位。有一个职位，是全职，还有一个是按需的。

00:48:31.630 --> 00:48:33.390
<v Robyn Semien>The full-time job went to someone else.
<v Robyn Semien>全职工作给了别人。

00:48:33.390 --> 00:49:00.100
<v Jason Kessler>So they hired me for the as-needed thing. And I was happy about that at first, because I'd been applying for these jobs for years after I graduated, and they wouldn't even give me interviews. So when I showed up to the orientation, I met the woman who had been hired over me, you know, for the full-time position. And we were all talking in this big orientation session about our experience.
<v Jason Kessler>所以他们雇佣我做了按需的工作。起初我很开心，因为我毕业后申请这些工作很多年了，他们甚至不会给我面试。所以当我出现在入职培训时，我遇到了那个被雇佣在我之上的女人，你知道，为了全职职位。我们都在谈论关于我们经验的这个大入职培训课程。

00:49:00.100 --> 00:49:37.020
<v Jason Kessler>She said that she had no experience in the mental health care field. She had worked as a dental hygienist. So at that point, I was pretty angry. Because I felt like I had been picked over for reasons not having anything to do with my qualifications, but having to do with identity variables. Why did I think this? Because most all the people working at this mental health care facility were white women or black women. I mean, there was no-- nothing else. It seemed to me like there was a discrimination that was happening there.
<v Jason Kessler>她说她在心理健康护理领域没有经验。她曾经做过牙科保健员。所以在那一刻，我非常生气。因为我觉得我被选中是因为与我的资历无关的原因，但与身份变量有关。为什么我认为这个？因为在这个心理健康护理机构工作的大多数人都是白人女性或黑人女性。我的意思是，没有——没有别的。在我看来，好像那里正在发生歧视。

00:49:37.020 --> 00:49:39.360
<v Robyn Semien>Was the woman who was picked over you, was she white or black?
<v Robyn Semien>那个被选中在你之上的女人，她是白人还是黑人？

00:49:39.360 --> 00:49:47.390
<v Jason Kessler>She was white. So I think-- I mean, the affirmative action stuff is not just about the race. It's also about the gender issue.
<v Jason Kessler>她是白人。所以我认为——我的意思是，肯定性行动的东西不仅仅是关于种族。它也是关于性别的问题。

00:49:47.390 --> 00:50:02.190
<v Robyn Semien>I suggested, maybe it wasn't discrimination. Maybe the woman was a better interview, or some other thing you didn't know about. But he decided-- discrimination, something fishy, even though he admitted he didn't have proof.
<v Robyn Semien>我建议，也许那不是歧视。也许那个女人是一个更好的面试者，或者是你不知道的某件其他事情。但他决定——歧视，有什么鱼腥味，尽管他承认他没有证据。

00:50:02.190 --> 00:50:08.340
<v Robyn Semien>OK, so you became convinced that that was, like, an injustice. And then you went on to work with her, right? How was she?
<v Robyn Semien>好的，所以你变得相信那是一个不公正。然后你继续和她工作，对吧？她怎么样？

00:50:08.340 --> 00:50:36.900
<v Jason Kessler>No, no, I didn't work at that job, because I found out that the hours weren't what I thought they would be. It was only like five hours a week. That's not enough to do anything. And there was no health care, no benefits. So I declined. I wouldn't work a job where, you know, I was clearly more qualified than somebody but had been discriminated against because of some variable outside my control.
<v Jason Kessler>不，不，我没有在那份工作上工作，因为我发现工作时间不是我想的那样。那只有每周5个小时左右。做任何事情都不够。而且没有医疗，没有福利。所以我拒绝了。我不会做一份工作，你知道，我明明比别人更有资历，但却因为我控制不了的某个变量而受到歧视。

00:50:36.900 --> 00:51:09.890
<v Robyn Semien>Of course, even if he were right, this is a story that happens much more often to women and people of color than white men. But years later, this is what Kessler points to as the beginning of a real shift. Slighted as a man, feeling like women and blacks get all the breaks, this story about being pushed out made sense to him, and it stuck. And this brings me to the thing I learned from this conversation. From there, he made a leap I truly can't understand, a leap from that experience back then to this conclusion.
<v Robyn Semien>当然，即使他是对的，这是一个比白人男性更常发生在女性和有色人种身上的故事。但多年后，这就是凯斯勒指向作为一个真正转变开始的事情。作为一个男人受到轻视，觉得女人和黑人得到了所有的突破，这个关于被排挤的故事对他有意义，并且它留下来了。这把我带到了我从这次谈话中学到的事情。从那里，他做了一个我真的无法理解的跳跃，一个从那时那种经历回到这个结论的跳跃。

00:51:09.890 --> 00:51:14.130
<v Jason Kessler>We are being replaced culturally and ethnically.
<v Jason Kessler>我们正在被文化上和种族上取代。

00:51:14.130 --> 00:51:19.280
<v Robyn Semien>You think there's, like, a government conspiracy to replace white people culturally?
<v Robyn Semien>你认为有一个，比如，政府阴谋来取代白人文化？

00:51:19.280 --> 00:51:38.330
<v Jason Kessler>No, I didn't say it was a conspiracy. It is something that is happening. It's a genocide by replacement. That's part of the UN definition of genocide, is replacing a people. And that is what they are doing. They created policies starting with the 1965 Hart-Cellar Immigration Act, which gave--
<v Jason Kessler>不，我没说它是阴谋。它是正在发生的事情。它是通过取代进行的种族灭绝。那是联合国定义的种族灭绝的一部分，是取代一个民族。而那就是他们正在做的。他们创造了政策，从1965年的《哈特-塞勒移民法》（Hart-Cellar Immigration Act）开始，它给了——

00:51:38.330 --> 00:52:07.630
<v Robyn Semien>The Hart-Cellar Act is the immigration policy that ended preferential treatment for certain European countries. It got rid of the old policy, which favored white immigrants. He's wrong, by the way, about the UN definition of genocide. It's not replacement. Genocide, according to the UN, is violent. It's the physical destruction of a people. But the way Kessler sees it, there's a genocide of white people, because America allows immigrants from countries all over the world.
<v Robyn Semien>《哈特-塞勒法》是结束某些欧洲国家优待待遇的移民政策。它摆脱了旧的政策，即偏袒白人移民。顺便说一句，关于联合国种族灭绝的定义，他是错的。这不是取代。种族灭绝，根据联合国，是暴力的。它是对一个民族的肉体毁灭。但以凯斯勒的方式看，有一个白人种族灭绝，因为美国允许来自世界各地的移民。

00:52:07.630 --> 00:52:14.380
<v Jason Kessler>This kind of thing doesn't happen in Africa, or Latin America, or Asia. It's only in the white countries.
<v Jason Kessler>这种事情在非洲，或拉丁美洲，或亚洲不会发生。它只在白人国家。

00:52:14.380 --> 00:52:17.440
<v Robyn Semien>I don't consider America a white country?
<v Robyn Semien>我不认为美国是一个白人国家？

00:52:17.440 --> 00:52:38.860
<v Jason Kessler>Why wouldn't you? It's been traditionally 80% to 85% white. Our first immigration policy, you know, said that in order to be a citizen you had to be a white person of good character, right? So it was explicitly a white country. Like, there are traditional demographics. And when you don't respect those, you destroy a people.
<v Jason Kessler>你为什么不这样认为？它传统上80%到85%是白人。我们的第一个移民政策，你知道，说了为了成为公民你必须是一个品行良好的白人，对吧？所以它明确是一个白人国家。像，有传统的人口统计。而当你尊重那些时，你摧毁了一个民族。

00:52:38.860 --> 00:53:17.200
<v Robyn Semien>"Destroy a people," "genocide." I'd never heard that before. I couldn't have guessed it. This is the new thing I learned from this conversation. But apparently this is something lots of these groups believe in. It came up 2 and 1/2 hours in, and it kind of washed past me when it happened. But later I'd mention it almost immediately to every person I talked to about him. The man who organized the rally in Charlottesville believes there's a genocide of white people in America.
<v Robyn Semien>“摧毁一个民族”，“种族灭绝”。我以前从未听说过。我无法猜到。这是我从这次谈话中学到的新东西。但显然这是很多这些团体相信的事情。它在2个半小时内出现，当它发生时，它有点像从我身边洗过。但后来我会几乎立即向我谈论他的每一个人提到它。夏洛茨维尔集会的组织者相信在美国有白人种族灭绝。

00:53:17.200 --> 00:53:42.780
<v Robyn Semien>White civil rights, discrimination against white people, white oppression-- Kessler is constantly stealing the language of the truly disenfranchised to talk about his feelings of white disenfranchisement. He calls people racist all the time. He calls Wes Bellamy a black supremacist. He calls me a racist toward white people. I have no idea if he knew I'm black, by the way.
<v Robyn Semien>白人公民权利，对白人的歧视，白人压迫——凯斯勒一直在窃取真正被剥夺权利者的语言来谈论他关于白人被剥夺权利的感觉。他一直称人们为种族主义者。他称韦斯·贝拉米为黑人至上主义者。他称我为对白人有种族歧视。顺便说一句，我不知道他是否知道我是黑人。

00:53:42.780 --> 00:54:20.070
<v Robyn Semien>That language is designed to have power. It took black Americans centuries to exact, black people who needed to communicate a message for our own survival, that we are not partial people, we are not a threat, we are not marginal, we are not invisible. We're people. Those words have power, the words Jason has co-opted. And they get results. I bet they'll get results for his movement too.
<v Robyn Semien>那种语言旨在拥有力量。黑人美国人花了几个世纪才精确化，黑人需要为我们自己的生存传达一个信息，我们不是部分的人，我们不是威胁，我们不是边缘，我们不是隐形的。我们是人。那些词拥有力量，杰森盗用的词。而且它们得到了结果。我打赌它们也会为他的运动得到结果。

00:54:20.070 --> 00:54:38.600
<v Robyn Semien>We talked for four hours in two conversations. Jason Kessler says he's not a white supremacist, but he believes in white genocide. And that's just as terrifying, because it takes you to the exact same place. Namely, taking back what's yours. I told Jason as much.
<v Robyn Semien>我们在两次谈话中谈了四个小时。杰森·凯斯勒说他不是白人至上主义者，但他相信白人种族灭绝。而那同样可怕，因为它带你去了完全相同的地方。即，拿回属于你的东西。我告诉杰森了。

00:54:38.600 --> 00:54:53.160
<v Robyn Semien>The reason that it's scary is that I don't think that even you are sure how far in over your head you are. And you're kind of being coy about-- like, you know the definition of a white supremacist, but you're not totally separating yourself from white supremacy.
<v Robyn Semien>它很可怕的原因是，我不认为连你自己都确定你陷得有多深。而且你有点在关于——比如，你知道白人至上主义者的定义，但你并没有完全把自己从白人至上主义中分离出来。

00:54:53.160 --> 00:54:53.780
<v Jason Kessler>Was Heather Heyer completely innocent?
<v Jason Kessler>希瑟·海耶（Heather Heyer）是完全无辜的吗？

00:54:53.780 --> 00:54:56.240
<v Robyn Semien>And white supremacy has a history of murder and killing.
<v Robyn Semien>而白人至上主义有谋杀和杀戮的历史。

00:54:56.240 --> 00:55:07.350
<v Jason Kessler>White supremacy, blah, blah, blah. That's a BS liberal term that you have been indoctrinated with. That "white supremacy, white supremacy!" This is our country!
<v Jason Kessler>白人至上主义，blah，blah，blah。那是你被灌输的一个胡说八道的自由派术语。那个“白人至上主义，白人至上主义！”这是我们的国家！

00:55:07.350 --> 00:55:38.450
<v Robyn Semien>After talking to Jason Kessler suddenly this past year in America looked different to me. I thought for lots of people "Make America Great Again" and white attachment to Confederate symbols was about white power, white people trying to maintain their power. Just a way of saying "remember the hierarchy. Don't forget who's on top." But Jason Kessler is a guy who does not believe he has power, and he wants it. Which is frightening, because it's unclear what he'll do to get it.
<v Robyn Semien>在与杰森·凯斯勒谈话后，美国过去这一年突然在我看来变得不同了。我以为对很多人来说，“让美国再次伟大”和白人对联盟符号的依恋是关于白人权力，白人试图维持他们的权力。只是说“记住等级制度。不要忘记谁在上面”的一种方式。但杰森·凯斯勒是一个不相信自己有权力的人，他想要它。这很可怕，因为不清楚为了得到它他会做什么。

00:55:38.450 --> 00:55:53.350
<v Robyn Semien>He's tweeted about a coming civil war and being one of the first to go. There was one question that I wanted to ask Kessler that I never got to, because he cut the conversation short. So I called him back one more time to try and ask him.
<v Robyn Semien>他发推文关于即将到来的内战并成为第一批走的人。我有一个想要问凯斯勒的问题，我从来没能问，因为他提前结束了谈话。所以我再次给他打了电话试着问他。

00:55:53.350 --> 00:55:59.960
<v Answering Machine>At the tone, please record your message. When you've finished recording, you may hang up or press one for more options.
<v Answering Machine>在提示音后，请记录你的信息。当你录完音后，你可以挂断或按一获取更多选项。

00:55:59.960 --> 00:56:00.410
<v Answering Machine>[BEEP]
<v Answering Machine>（嘟声）

00:56:00.410 --> 00:56:20.900
<v Robyn Semien>Hey, Jason. It's Robyn. I didn't get to ask you what you wanted, what your movement was looking for in the future. Is what you're hoping for a civil war, like a race war? OK, let me know. Bye.
<v Robyn Semien>嘿，杰森。我是罗宾。我没能问你想要什么，你的运动未来在寻找什么。你所希望的是内战，像种族战争吗？好的，让我知道。再见。

00:56:20.900 --> 00:56:23.230
<v Robyn Semien>[CLICK]
<v Robyn Semien>（点击）

00:56:23.230 --> 00:56:55.470
<v Ira Glass>He didn't call me back. When I sent an email following up this week, asking, "Do you want a race war? What will that look like?" He responded, quote, "Ridiculous. Just run what you have. I'm not a cartoon villain." I never thought he was.
<v Ira Glass>他没回我电话。当我本周发电子邮件跟进时，问，“你想要种族战争吗？那会是什么样子？”他回应，引用，“荒谬。只管运行你拥有的东西。我不是卡通反派。”我从来没认为他是。

00:56:55.470 --> 00:57:28.660
<v Ira Glass>Robyn Semien is one of the producers of our show.
<v Ira Glass>罗宾·塞米恩是我们节目的制作人之一。

00:57:28.660 --> 00:57:47.750
<v Ira Glass>Our program was produced today by our senior producer, Brian Reed. People who put our show together include Elna Baker, Elise Bergerson, Susan Burton, Zoe Chace, Dana Chivvis, Stephanie Foo, Michele Harris, Kimberly Henderson, Chana Joffe-Walt, Mariya Karimjiee, David Kestenbaum, Seth Lind, Miki Meek, Jonathan Menjivar, BA Parker, Robyn Semien, Christopher Swetala, Matt Tierney, Nancy Updike, Julie Whitaker, and Diane Wu.
<v Ira Glass>我们的节目今天由我们的资深制作人布莱恩·里德（Brian Reed）制作。把我们节目放在一起的人包括艾尔娜·贝克（Elna Baker），艾莉丝·伯格森（Elise Bergerson），苏珊·伯顿（Susan Burton），佐伊·蔡斯，达纳·奇维斯（Dana Chivvis），斯蒂芬妮·福（Stephanie Foo），米歇尔·哈里斯（Michele Harris），金伯利·亨德森（Kimberly Henderson），查娜·乔夫-沃尔特（Chana Joffe-Walt），玛丽亚·卡里姆吉（Mariya Karimjiee），戴维·克斯滕鲍姆（David Kestenbaum），塞斯·林德（Seth Lind），米基·米克（Miki Meek），乔纳森·门吉瓦尔（Jonathan Menjivar），BA帕克（BA Parker），罗宾·塞米恩，克里斯托弗·斯韦塔拉（Christopher Swetala），马特·蒂尔尼（Matt Tierney），南希·厄普代克（Nancy Updike），朱莉·惠特克（Julie Whitaker），和黛安·吴（Diane Wu）。

00:57:47.750 --> 00:57:56.070
